Analysis: It doesn't. Proposal 0 suggests adding `onModalWillHide` to `ConfirmModal` and `BaseModal` (which is already there for `BaseModal`). Then they suggest passing it to `ReactNativeModal` (already done). 

Let's analyze Proposal 4: Add `Keyboard.dismiss()` to `ContactMethodDetailsPage.js` inside the `cancel` function (or somewhere else). The proposal says: "First we have to add Keybord.dismiss() here. we are not focusing on input again after pressing cancel this will also make things consistent on all platforms. disable KeyboardAvoidingView in ContactMethodsPage.js". Disabling `KeyboardAvoidingView` is almost certainly a bad idea because it affects the whole page.

Let's analyze Proposal 1: 
Wait, does `Keyboard.dismiss()` inside `onModalWillHide` work? The problem is that when `deleteContactMethod` is called, it immediately navigates back to `ContactMethodsPage`. When `deleteContactMethod` is called, the modal is hidden. 
Actually, `ContactMethodDetailsPage.tsx` has:
```javascript
    const confirmDeleteAndHideModal = useCallback(() => {
        toggleDeleteModal(false);
        deleteContactMethod(contactMethod, loginList ?? {}, backTo);
    }, [contactMethod, loginList, toggleDeleteModal, backTo]);
```
If `deleteContactMethod` navigates back immediately, the modal will unmount or hide. 
If we use `Keyboard.addListener('keyboardDidHide', ...)` before opening the modal, that ensures the keyboard is fully hidden before the modal appears. 
Wait, the issue states: "keyboard open again for a second when user click on remove button"
Steps:
1. Go to settings > profile > contact method
2. Click on new contact method
3. Enter any email and click on Add
4. Click on email
5. Open the keyboard if it's not open
6. click on remove button and confirm button

Ah! The issue is when you click the "remove" button and then "confirm" button. 
Wait! 
When you click on the "remove" button (which is the trash can icon), the modal opens. 
If the keyboard is open, clicking the "remove" button (which is a menu item) will open the modal. On iOS, opening a modal automatically dismisses the keyboard, but when the modal closes, the keyboard might try to reappear if the input was still focused.
In `ContactMethodDetailsPage.tsx`, when you click "remove", the modal opens. Then you click "confirm". 
In `confirmDeleteAndHideModal`, it calls `toggleDeleteModal(false)` and `deleteContactMethod(...)`.
`deleteContactMethod` immediately calls `Navigation.goBack()`. 
Since the input is still focused (because we didn't explicitly dismiss the keyboard, or we did but iOS tries to restore focus), when the modal closes, iOS tries to restore the keyboard. But we are also navigating back. So the keyboard pops up for a second on the new screen.

Proposal 0 says: "We are not dismissing the keyboard when the delete confirm modal opens. It just gets hidden due to the modal but after modal closes, it re-appears on the other screen. ... We should add onModalWillHide prop to ConfirmModal and BaseModal which will pass to ReactNativeModal. This prop will help us schedule a callback just before the modal hides to dismiss the keyboard by calling Keyboard.dismiss()"
Wait, if the keyboard is hidden when the modal opens, why does calling `Keyboard.dismiss()` on modal close help? Because iOS remembers the focused input and restores the keyboard when the modal closes. Explicitly calling `Keyboard.dismiss()` clears the focus.

Wait, Proposal 4 says: "we are not focusing on input again after pressing cancel this will also make things consistent on all platforms". 
Let's look at `ContactMethodDetailsPage.tsx` around line 223.
```javascript
            onCancel={() => toggleDeleteModal(false)}
            onModalHide={() => {
                InteractionManager.runAfterInteractions(() => {
                    validateCodeFormRef.current?.focusLastSelected?.();
                });
            }}
```
Ah!!! 
`onModalHide` is currently defined as:
```javascript
            onModalHide={() => {
                InteractionManager.runAfterInteractions(() => {
                    validateCodeFormRef.current?.focusLastSelected?.();
                });
            }}
```
This means whenever the modal hides (whether confirmed or cancelled), it focuses the input!
Let me verify this.
Plan: I will examine `ContactMethodDetailsPage.tsx` at `getDeleteConfirmationModal` to see the `onModalHide` prop.

